  "name": "Question 1",
  "problem": "Consider the function $f(x)=2+3x-{e}^{-x}.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $f(x)$ is concave down on $(-\\infty ,\\infty )$\n- $f(x)$ is concave up on $(0,\\infty )$\n- $f(x)$ is concave up on $(-\\infty ,0)$",
  "worked_solution": "First, we calculate the second derivative of $f(x),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{\u2032}(x) & =3+{e}^{-x} \\\\ {f}^{\u2033}(x) & =-{e}^{-x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince ${e}^{-x}>0$ for every real value of $x,$ it follows that $-{e}^{-x}<0$ for every real value of $x.$ Therefore, ${f}^{\u2033}(x)$ is never positive, so there are no intervals where $f(x)$ is concave up.\n\nSince ${f}^{\u2033}(x)<0$ for every real value of $x,$ the function is concave down on $(-\\infty ,\\infty ).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"",
